## What complexity actually means

Complexity isn't about seconds. It's about how the number of operations grows as the input grows.

If your code does roughly f(n) basic operations, then:

Doubling n in O(n) → work doubles
Doubling n in O(n²) → work becomes 4×
Increasing n 10× in O(n²) → work becomes 100×

In 1 second it can do about 10⁸ operations
≈ 10⁸ simple operations per second on a typical modern competitive-programming environment.
So you can roughly estimate:
Operations
Approx. time
10⁶ -> 0.01 sec
10⁷ -> 0.1 sec
10⁸ -> 1 sec
10⁹ -> 10 sec

Cheat sheet: constraint → feasible complexity
Max n	Feasible complexity	Typical techniques
≤ 10-11           ->   	O(n!) Brute-force permutations
≤ 20-25           ->   	O(2ⁿ), O(2ⁿ · n)  Bitmask, subsets, backtracking
≤ 40	          ->   	O(2^(n/2)) 	Meet in the middle
≤ 100             ->   	O(n⁴)  Nested DP, Floyd-Warshall-ish
≤ 500	          ->    O(n³)  Floyd-Warshall, matrix-style DP
≤ 5,000	          ->    O(n²)  2D DP, all pairs
≤ 10⁵ to 2×10⁵    ->   	O(n log n) Sorting, segment tree, binary search, heap
≤ 10⁶	          ->	O(n) or O(n log n) (tight)	Linear scan, two pointers, prefix sums
≤ 10⁸	          ->    O(n)  very light	Sieve, simple loop
≤ 10¹⁸	          ->   	O(log n) or O(1) 	Binary search on answer, math, fast exponentiation

How to use it: read the constraint, find the row, and that tells you what the intended solution looks like. Setters choose constraints deliberately to rule out slower approaches.

 Reading constraints as hints

Constraints are basically the problem telling you the answer's shape:

n ≤ 20 → the solution is exponential (bitmask / brute force).
n ≤ 5000 → the intended solution is O(n²), often a 2D DP.
n ≤ 10⁵ or 2×10⁵ → O(n log n): think sorting, binary search, heaps, segment trees, or a clever O(n).
n ≤ 10⁶ or more → strictly linear: single pass, hashing, prefix sums, sieve.
n up to 10⁹ or 10¹⁸ → you can't even loop. Think math, formulas, binary search on the answer, or O(log n).
Two constraints (e.g., n, m ≤ 1000) → complexity might be O(n·m) = 10⁶, which is fine.
Multiple test cases → the limit usually applies to the sum of n across all tests. Check this carefully, because it changes what's feasible.

Counting Complexity
Sequential blocks → add (keep the biggest)
Nested loops → multiply
Halving each step → log n
for j = i; j ≤ n; j += i → n/i steps → total O(n log n) (harmonic series)
for j = i..n inside for i = 1..n → n(n+1)/2 → O(n²)
Recursion → (number of calls) × (work per call); 2 branches, depth n → O(2ⁿ)
Common Operation Costs
Sort → O(n log n)
Hash map lookup → O(1) average
Heap / set / map op → O(log n)
list.remove() / insert at front → O(n)
String concat in loop → O(n²)
Binary search → O(log n)

Fixes for Repeated Work
Problem	Fix
Repeated range sums	prefix sum
Repeated searching	sort + binary search / hash map
Checking pairs	two pointers / sliding window
Overlapping subproblems	DP / memoization
Repeated min/max/updates	heap, segment tree, Fenwick

Space complexity intuition

Same idea, but counting memory instead of operations. Typical limit is 256 MB.

An int is 4 bytes, a long long is 8 bytes.
256 MB ≈ 6 × 10⁷ ints (a rough ceiling; stay well below).
A 1D array of 10⁶ ints = 4 MB → fine.
A 2D array of 10⁴ × 10⁴ = 10⁸ ints = 400 MB → too much.
A 2D array of 5000 × 5000 ints = 100 MB → okay, but tight with other arrays.
